{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01VKDVZAHwDsVNvEdAhkVt7P","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The file has been written. Here's a summary of what's in each question:\n\n---\n\n## What was written\n\n**`04-real-time-updates-webhooks.md`** — 10 interview-depth answers covering the full real-time payments surface:\n\n### Core (7 questions)\n\n| Q | Topic | Key substance |\n|---|-------|---------------|\n| Q1 | `confirmCardPayment` vs. webhook reconciliation | Why the client-side return isn't ground truth; the \"verifying\" intermediate state; when it's safe to show success; sequence diagram of the full flow |\n| Q2 | SSE vs. WebSocket vs. polling comparison | Full comparison table across 8 dimensions; why SSE wins for payment confirmation (unidirectional, native reconnect, HTTP semantics); working SSE client + server code |\n| Q3 | Resilient polling with exponential backoff | Full implementation with `AbortController` cleanup; jitter formula; attempt schedule table (0s → 1s → 2s → 4s → cap 30s); UX copy at each stage |\n| Q4 | Client-side attack scenario | Step-by-step fake-success attack walkthrough; why `pi_id` being real makes it exploitable; vulnerable vs. correct server patterns; race condition exploit via `processing` status |\n| Q5 | \"Processing\" state UX for async payment methods | Screen copy for bank transfers/SEPA/ACH; timer-based phase progression by payment method; order created immediately in `payment_pending`; email notification contract; flowchart for browser-close recovery |\n| Q6 | Re-surfacing outcome after user navigates away | `pendingPayments` DB table schema; webhook handler populates `notified: false`; per-user query on page load with Redis cache; localStorage fallback for guests |\n| Q7 | Webhook arrives while UI shows failure | SSE stays open after error (5-min grace period); SSE overrides UI state; explicit \"Good news\" modal to explain the correction |\n\n### Deep (3 questions)\n\n| Q | Topic | Key substance |\n|---|-------|---------------|\n| Q8 | Multi-context notification system | Full data flow for all 3 cases (active tab / reopened tab / mobile background); `Last-Event-ID` replay mechanism; Redis pub/sub for horizontal scaling; APNs/FCM for background mobile; event deduplication architecture |\n| Q9 | Webhook → frontend consistency pipeline | Why DB-read-before-push prevents stale intermediate states; transactional webhook processing with idempotency; return 200 to Stripe immediately, process async; sequence diagram with every step annotated |\n| Q10 | POS dashboard for concurrent payments | Full WebSocket message schema with `sequenceNumber` and `patch` semantics; Zustand normalized `Map<orderId, Order>` store; per-order `React.memo` with equality selector; `react-window` virtualization; reconnection with server-side event replay; out-of-order rejection via sequence numbers |"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ad975417d8cabbc9b (use SendMessage with to: 'ad975417d8cabbc9b' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 61547\ntool_uses: 3\nduration_ms: 280999</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c680bfc-f8ab-4d28-9211-0306b83e7a73","uuid":"ac4b910a-569d-4235-8214-3f766e528126","timestamp":"2026-06-20T19:03:29.896Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Write a comprehensive interview-prep answer file for the payments-system topic, section 04: Real-Time Updates & Webhooks.\n\nOutput file: /Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/payments-system/answers/04-real-time-updates-webhooks.md\n\n## Format requirements\n- Follow the exact format used in existing answer files (read 03-state-management-transaction-lifecycle.md to match structure)\n- Each question gets: **Problem framing** → **Approach** → **Tradeoffs**\n- Use Mermaid diagrams (```mermaid) where they add clarity — especially for flows/sequences\n- Depth should be interview-caliber: specific, technical, concrete — not hand-wavy\n\n## Questions to answer (from design doc section 04)\n\n### Core (7 questions)\n\n1. After calling `stripe.confirmCardPayment()`, Stripe returns immediately with a `PaymentIntent` object. But the actual charge confirmation comes asynchronously via webhook. How do you reconcile the frontend's view of payment status with the authoritative server-side status, and when is it safe to show the user a success screen?\n\n2. Compare polling, WebSockets, and Server-Sent Events (SSE) as mechanisms for delivering payment status updates to the frontend. Which would you choose for a payment confirmation flow, and why?\n\n3. How would you implement a polling strategy for payment status that is resilient to transient network failures, doesn't hammer your server, and gives up gracefully after a timeout? Describe the backoff algorithm and the UX at each stage.\n\n4. What is the risk of relying solely on the browser-side `stripe.confirmCardPayment()` return value to determine payment success, rather than verifying server-side via webhook? Give a specific attack scenario where this leads to revenue loss or fraud.\n\n5. Describe the \"pending payment\" screen UX for a payment that enters `processing` state (common with bank transfers, SEPA debit, or certain card networks). What do you show the user, how long do you keep them on this screen, and what happens when they close the browser?\n\n6. How do you handle a scenario where a webhook arrives at your server but the frontend user has already navigated away? How do you re-surface the payment outcome the next time the user visits?\n\n7. Your server receives a `payment_intent.succeeded` webhook but the frontend is currently showing the user a \"payment failed\" error screen (because the initial API call timed out). How do you reconcile this and correct the UI?\n\n### Deep (3 questions)\n\n8. Design a real-time payment status notification system that must work across: (a) an active checkout tab using SSE, (b) a user who has closed the tab but re-opens the site within 10 minutes, and (c) a mobile user who has backgrounded the app. Describe the full data flow from Stripe webhook → your backend → frontend for each case, including how you handle reconnection, missed events, and event deduplication.\n\n9. Stripe sends webhooks with a `Stripe-Signature` header for verification. Your frontend needs to display a real-time status update the moment this webhook is processed. Walk through the full architecture: webhook receiver → verification → event storage → push to frontend. How do you ensure the frontend update is consistent with the database state and doesn't display a stale intermediate state?\n\n10. You are building a payment dashboard that shows live transaction status for multiple concurrent payments (think a restaurant POS processing 20 orders simultaneously). Describe how you would design the WebSocket message schema, client-side store normalization, and render optimization to ensure the UI stays responsive and correct as events arrive out of order.\n\n## Reference files to read first\n\n1. Read /Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/payments-system/answers/03-state-management-transaction-lifecycle.md to match the exact format and depth\n2. Read /Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/payments-system/answers/01-checkout-flow-form-ux.md to understand style\n\n## Key technical substance to include\n\n- Stripe confirmCardPayment returns synchronously but webhook is ground truth; reconciliation window\n- SSE vs WebSocket vs polling: SSE is simpler (HTTP/2, built-in reconnect), WS for bidirectional; polling with exponential backoff for fallback\n- Polling: exponential backoff (1s → 2s → 4s → 8s → cap at 30s), jitter, max attempts (e.g. 12 attempts = ~2 min), AbortController for cleanup\n- Client-secret-based attack: attacker captures confirmCardPayment success client-side, backend never processes charge → ship without goods\n- Processing state UX: show email confirmation info, don't keep user waiting, use async notification (email/push) for eventual settlement\n- Re-surface: store pending payment_intent_id in session/DB, check on next page load/login, show banner \"Your payment is being processed\"\n- Timeout + later webhook: use SSE/WebSocket push OR polling on confirmation page; when webhook arrives, push event to open tabs; for closed tabs, persist state and re-surface on next visit\n- Multi-case SSE system: SSE for active tab, DB poll on reopen, push notification for mobile background\n- Webhook → frontend pipeline: verify signature → write to DB → publish to pub/sub → SSE/WS push; consistency via DB-read-before-push\n- POS dashboard: WebSocket schema with order_id, status, timestamp; normalized store (Map<orderId, Order>); React.memo + virtualization for 20+ concurrent orders; out-of-order handling via sequence numbers or timestamps\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n# 04 — Real-Time Updates & Webhooks\n\n[Brief intro paragraph about why this section matters]\n\n---\n\n## Core Questions\n\n### Q1. [question title — ~5 words]\n\n**Problem framing:** ...\n\n**Approach:** ...\n\n[mermaid diagram if useful]\n\n**Tradeoffs:** ...\n\n---\n\n[repeat for all 7 core + 3 deep questions]\n```\n\nWrite the full file now. Be thorough — this is interview prep material where depth matters.","agentId":"ad975417d8cabbc9b","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The file has been written. Here's a summary of what's in each question:\n\n---\n\n## What was written\n\n**`04-real-time-updates-webhooks.md`** — 10 interview-depth answers covering the full real-time payments surface:\n\n### Core (7 questions)\n\n| Q | Topic | Key substance |\n|---|-------|---------------|\n| Q1 | `confirmCardPayment` vs. webhook reconciliation | Why the client-side return isn't ground truth; the \"verifying\" intermediate state; when it's safe to show success; sequence diagram of the full flow |\n| Q2 | SSE vs. WebSocket vs. polling comparison | Full comparison table across 8 dimensions; why SSE wins for payment confirmation (unidirectional, native reconnect, HTTP semantics); working SSE client + server code |\n| Q3 | Resilient polling with exponential backoff | Full implementation with `AbortController` cleanup; jitter formula; attempt schedule table (0s → 1s → 2s → 4s → cap 30s); UX copy at each stage |\n| Q4 | Client-side attack scenario | Step-by-step fake-success attack walkthrough; why `pi_id` being real makes it exploitable; vulnerable vs. correct server patterns; race condition exploit via `processing` status |\n| Q5 | \"Processing\" state UX for async payment methods | Screen copy for bank transfers/SEPA/ACH; timer-based phase progression by payment method; order created immediately in `payment_pending`; email notification contract; flowchart for browser-close recovery |\n| Q6 | Re-surfacing outcome after user navigates away | `pendingPayments` DB table schema; webhook handler populates `notified: false`; per-user query on page load with Redis cache; localStorage fallback for guests |\n| Q7 | Webhook arrives while UI shows failure | SSE stays open after error (5-min grace period); SSE overrides UI state; explicit \"Good news\" modal to explain the correction |\n\n### Deep (3 questions)\n\n| Q | Topic | Key substance |\n|---|-------|---------------|\n| Q8 | Multi-context notification system | Full data flow for all 3 cases (active tab / reopened tab / mobile background); `Last-Event-ID` replay mechanism; Redis pub/sub for horizontal scaling; APNs/FCM for background mobile; event deduplication architecture |\n| Q9 | Webhook → frontend consistency pipeline | Why DB-read-before-push prevents stale intermediate states; transactional webhook processing with idempotency; return 200 to Stripe immediately, process async; sequence diagram with every step annotated |\n| Q10 | POS dashboard for concurrent payments | Full WebSocket message schema with `sequenceNumber` and `patch` semantics; Zustand normalized `Map<orderId, Order>` store; per-order `React.memo` with equality selector; `react-window` virtualization; reconnection with server-side event replay; out-of-order rejection via sequence numbers |"}],"resolvedModel":"blackboxai/anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5","totalDurationMs":280999,"totalTokens":61547,"totalToolUseCount":3,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24223,"cache_read_input_tokens":36622,"output_tokens":701,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":24223},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":2,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":1,"linesAdded":1233,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
