{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_018ezfxqc48FqE1h98sZBMct","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# 04 — Real-Time Updates & Webhooks\n2\t\n3\tPayment status is never instantaneous. Stripe's `confirmCardPayment` returns within milliseconds, but the authoritative settlement signal — the webhook — arrives asynchronously, sometimes seconds later, sometimes minutes, and for certain payment methods like SEPA debit or ACH, days. This gap between \"the user clicked Pay\" and \"the bank confirmed the charge\" is where most real-time complexity lives. This section covers the full architecture of delivering payment status updates reliably across every frontend context: active tabs, closed-and-reopened tabs, mobile apps in the background, and concurrent POS dashboards processing dozens of payments simultaneously. The recurring theme is that the webhook is ground truth — everything on the client is provisional until the server confirms it.\n4\t\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t## Core Questions\n8\t\n9\t### Q1. Reconciling `confirmCardPayment` return vs. webhook ground truth\n10\t\n11\t**Problem framing:** `stripe.confirmCardPayment()` is a synchronous-feeling call that resolves with a `PaymentIntent` object whose `status` field says `succeeded`. This is not the same as the Stripe webhook event `payment_intent.succeeded`. The JS SDK call returns after Stripe's API has created and confirmed the charge object, but \"confirmed\" here means Stripe accepted the request and it is in flight — not that the issuing bank has settled it. The webhook arrives after the issuing bank communicates the final outcome back through the card network. For most cards this gap is milliseconds to a few seconds. For certain card programs (deferred debit, some international networks) the gap is longer, and in all cases the webhook is what your backend actually acts on to fulfill the order. If a user sees a success screen based only on the client-side return value but the webhook never arrives (network failure, Stripe retry exhausted, your endpoint returned 500), you may fulfill an order with no settled funds.\n12\t\n13\t**Approach:**\n14\t\n15\tThe reconciliation architecture works in two layers: client-side optimism gated by server confirmation.\n16\t\n17\t```mermaid\n18\tsequenceDiagram\n19\t    participant Browser\n20\t    participant YourAPI as Your Backend\n21\t    participant Stripe\n22\t\n23\t    Browser->>Stripe: stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret)\n24\t    Stripe-->>Browser: PaymentIntent { status: \"succeeded\" }\n25\t    Note over Browser: DO NOT show success yet\n26\t    Browser->>YourAPI: POST /api/payment/confirm { paymentIntentId }\n27\t    Stripe->>YourAPI: POST /webhooks/stripe (payment_intent.succeeded)\n28\t    YourAPI->>YourAPI: Verify signature, write to DB, fulfill order\n29\t    YourAPI-->>Browser: { status: \"succeeded\", orderId: \"ord_xxx\" }\n30\t    Note over Browser: NOW show success screen\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\tAfter `confirmCardPayment` resolves with `status: \"succeeded\"`, immediately call your own backend to confirm:\n34\t\n35\t```javascript\n36\tasync function handlePaymentConfirmation(clientSecret, stripe) {\n37\t  const { paymentIntent, error } = await stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret, {\n38\t    payment_method: { card: cardElement },\n39\t  });\n40\t\n41\t  if (error) {\n42\t    return { outcome: 'failed', error };\n43\t  }\n44\t\n45\t  // Client-side says succeeded — now verify server-side\n46\t  // Show \"Confirming your payment...\" — not success yet\n47\t  setStatus('verifying');\n48\t\n49\t  const serverConfirmation = await pollServerStatus(paymentIntent.id, {\n50\t    maxAttempts: 12,\n51\t    initialDelayMs: 500,\n52\t  });\n53\t\n54\t  if (serverConfirmation.status === 'succeeded') {\n55\t    // Webhook was received and processed — safe to show success\n56\t    return { outcome: 'succeeded', orderId: serverConfirmation.orderId };\n57\t  }\n58\t\n59\t  return { outcome: 'processing', paymentIntentId: paymentIntent.id };\n60\t}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c680bfc-f8ab-4d28-9211-0306b83e7a73","uuid":"17730568-fe7f-4794-80af-018fcba8fb08","timestamp":"2026-06-20T19:03:33.275Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/payments-system/answers/04-real-time-updates-webhooks.md","content":"# 04 — Real-Time Updates & Webhooks\n\nPayment status is never instantaneous. Stripe's `confirmCardPayment` returns within milliseconds, but the authoritative settlement signal — the webhook — arrives asynchronously, sometimes seconds later, sometimes minutes, and for certain payment methods like SEPA debit or ACH, days. This gap between \"the user clicked Pay\" and \"the bank confirmed the charge\" is where most real-time complexity lives. This section covers the full architecture of delivering payment status updates reliably across every frontend context: active tabs, closed-and-reopened tabs, mobile apps in the background, and concurrent POS dashboards processing dozens of payments simultaneously. The recurring theme is that the webhook is ground truth — everything on the client is provisional until the server confirms it.\n\n---\n\n## Core Questions\n\n### Q1. Reconciling `confirmCardPayment` return vs. webhook ground truth\n\n**Problem framing:** `stripe.confirmCardPayment()` is a synchronous-feeling call that resolves with a `PaymentIntent` object whose `status` field says `succeeded`. This is not the same as the Stripe webhook event `payment_intent.succeeded`. The JS SDK call returns after Stripe's API has created and confirmed the charge object, but \"confirmed\" here means Stripe accepted the request and it is in flight — not that the issuing bank has settled it. The webhook arrives after the issuing bank communicates the final outcome back through the card network. For most cards this gap is milliseconds to a few seconds. For certain card programs (deferred debit, some international networks) the gap is longer, and in all cases the webhook is what your backend actually acts on to fulfill the order. If a user sees a success screen based only on the client-side return value but the webhook never arrives (network failure, Stripe retry exhausted, your endpoint returned 500), you may fulfill an order with no settled funds.\n\n**Approach:**\n\nThe reconciliation architecture works in two layers: client-side optimism gated by server confirmation.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Browser\n    participant YourAPI as Your Backend\n    participant Stripe\n\n    Browser->>Stripe: stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret)\n    Stripe-->>Browser: PaymentIntent { status: \"succeeded\" }\n    Note over Browser: DO NOT show success yet\n    Browser->>YourAPI: POST /api/payment/confirm { paymentIntentId }\n    Stripe->>YourAPI: POST /webhooks/stripe (payment_intent.succeeded)\n    YourAPI->>YourAPI: Verify signature, write to DB, fulfill order\n    YourAPI-->>Browser: { status: \"succeeded\", orderId: \"ord_xxx\" }\n    Note over Browser: NOW show success screen\n```\n\nAfter `confirmCardPayment` resolves with `status: \"succeeded\"`, immediately call your own backend to confirm:\n\n```javascript\nasync function handlePaymentConfirmation(clientSecret, stripe) {\n  const { paymentIntent, error } = await stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret, {\n    payment_method: { card: cardElement },\n  });\n\n  if (error) {\n    return { outcome: 'failed', error };\n  }\n\n  // Client-side says succeeded — now verify server-side\n  // Show \"Confirming your payment...\" — not success yet\n  setStatus('verifying');\n\n  const serverConfirmation = await pollServerStatus(paymentIntent.id, {\n    maxAttempts: 12,\n    initialDelayMs: 500,\n  });\n\n  if (serverConfirmation.status === 'succeeded') {\n    // Webhook was received and processed — safe to show success\n    return { outcome: 'succeeded', orderId: serverConfirmation.orderId };\n  }\n\n  return { outcome: 'processing', paymentIntentId: paymentIntent.id };\n}","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1233}}}
